{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_3vhrxqn5guljh3oj6qyef4zpzw55rg5wla47wdbuvju7qenco27a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. **The Rust translation is bit-exact; I found no divergence and changed no library code.**\n\n## What the library is\n\nOne C translation unit: a 145-entry `static const float g_pow43[]` table and `float pow43(int)`, which returns `x^(4/3)` — a table lookup for `x < 129`, otherwise a quadratic interpolation between table entries with a `mult` of 16 or 256.\n\n## Phases\n\n**A — Surface mapping.** `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (12 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (26 rows). Grepping the C found **no error mechanism at all**: no `assert`, no sentinel return, no range check, no pointers, no enums. So the rejection surface is entirely implicit — the unchecked subscript and the signed-overflow paths. I derived the exact well-defined domain (`x ∈ -16..=8223`) and *proved* division-by-zero unreachable rather than assuming it.\n\n**B — 26 differential tests**, one per config row, all through `dlopen`/`dlsym` on both `.so`s. Four rows are exhaustive, so the union covers **every** well-defined input (all 8240), not a sample.\n\n**C — 17 error-path tests.** The interesting part: outside the defined domain the C reads whatever the linker put after `.rodata`, so byte-equality is impossible there. Rather than write that off as \"UB, out of scope\", I made it observable three ways — a table-relative oracle (locating each object's private table via `/proc/self/maps`), synthetic `mmap`ed pages at exactly the address each subscript targets, and fault-parity via `fork`.\n\n**D —** Symbol diff empty; whole suite passes under every feature combination and a dev-profile `.so` with overflow checks.\n\n## Three findings worth flagging\n\n- **I initially fooled myself.** My first mutation run reported \"harness gaps\", but the patterns were hitting the doc-comment copy of the C source, not the code. Separately, a `SIGBUS` exposed that `/proc/self/maps` reports `.so` bytes past EOF as readable. I also caught my page-mapping test *interfering* with other tests — it appeared to catch two mutants but passed them when run alone, so I moved it to its own binary. Had I trusted the first green run, three conclusions would have been wrong.\n\n- **Exhaustive search found a 5-in-2-billion bug class.** Doing the `frac` division in `f64` (a very plausible mistranslation, since C's usual arithmetic conversions make it single-precision) changes `frac` for 264M inputs but the *output* for only 5 — all deep out-of-bounds. Those witnesses are now pinned. **20 of 20** non-equivalent mutants are detected, each by a differential FFI test.\n\n- **Two \"misses\" are provably equivalent, not gaps.** `x < 129` → `x < 128` is invisible because `g_pow43[144] == 16 * g_pow43[32]` bit-exactly in `f32`; the `2/9` literal substitution uses a genuinely different `f32` yet never changes `poly` for any `x` (verified exhaustively over 2³¹).\n\nOne caveat on the reference itself: the C diverges from its own baseline build by 24 values under `-march=x86-64-v3` (FMA contraction) and 2082 under `-Ofast`. `CMakeLists.txt` sets no flags, so baseline is the correct target — and Rust matches it under every `target-cpu`, since it never auto-contracts."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":853,"cache_read_input_tokens":187312,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":853,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c33bed92-1747-4f94-b4d1-217d8aeef992","uuid":"560babab-de1d-49a3-9848-be9ebf6c5343","timestamp":"2026-08-27T01:00:30.960Z"}
